#### C++ vs JavaScript
```cpp
// C++ - Synchronous, strongly typed, compiled
#include <thread>
#include <chrono>

int main() {
    auto fibonacci = [](int n) -> int {
        if (n <= 1) return n;
        return fibonacci(n-1) + fibonacci(n-2);  // ERROR: cannot capture lambda recursively
    };
    
    // Proper implementation
    function<int(int)> fib = [&fib](int n) -> int {
        return n <= 1 ? n : fib(n-1) + fib(n-2);
    };
    
    cout << fib(10) << endl;  // Blocks, waits for result
    return 0;
}
// Strongly typed, compile-time checking, fast
```

```javascript
// JavaScript - Asynchronous, dynamically typed, interpreted
const fibonacci = (n) => {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    return fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2);
};

setTimeout(() => {
    console.log(fibonacci(10));
}, 0);  // Runs asynchronously in event loop

console.log("Main continues");  // Runs before fibonacci result
// Dynamically typed, no compile-time checking, event-driven
```

**Comparison**:
| Aspect | C++ | JavaScript |
|--------|-----|-----------|
| Type System | Static, strong | Dynamic, loose |
| Execution | Synchronous, compiled | Asynchronous, interpreted |
| Concurrency | Multi-threading | Event loop, promises |
| Performance | Native speed | Interpreted (V8 JIT faster) |
| Memory | Manual (or smart pointers) | Automatic garbage collection |
| Use | Systems, games, backend | Browser scripting, Node.js |

#### C++ vs Rust
```cpp
// C++ - Manual memory management, trust the programmer
void unsafeExample() {
    int* ptr = new int(42);
    int* ptr2 = ptr;
    delete ptr;
    cout << *ptr2;  // UNDEFINED BEHAVIOR - use after free
}

// C++ allows this, no compile-time error
```

```rust
// Rust - Memory safety without garbage collection (Ownership model)
fn safe_example() {
    let mut x = 42;
    let ptr = &mut x;
    let ptr2 = &mut x;  // ERROR: cannot borrow x as mutable more than once
    println!("{}", ptr2);
}

// Rust prevents this at compile time
```

**Comparison**:
| Aspect | C++ | Rust |
|--------|-----|------|
| Memory Safety | Programmer responsibility | Compiler enforced |
| Data Races | Possible at runtime | Impossible (compiler prevents) |
| Null Pointers | Possible (nullptr) | Eliminated (Option<T>) |
| Performance | Fastest | Fastest (same as C++) |
| Learning Curve | Very steep | Extremely steep (borrow checker) |
| Compilation | Fast | Slower (intensive checking) |
| Ecosystem | Large, mature | Growing, smaller |

### Compilation Process
1. **Preprocessing**: Handles `#include`, `#define`
2. **Compilation**: Converts to assembly code
3. **Assembly**: Converts to object code (.obj)
4. **Linking**: Combines object files and libraries into executable

### Header Files vs Implementation
- **Headers (.h/.hpp)**: Declarations, class definitions
- **Implementation (.cpp)**: Definitions, function bodies
- **Purpose**: Separation of interface and implementation

