### Type Conversions

**Implicit Conversion Rules**:
```cpp
// Widening (safe, automatic)
char c = 'A';      // ASCII 65
short s = c;       // Implicit conversion: 65
int i = s;         // Implicit conversion: 65
long l = i;        // Implicit conversion: 65
float f = l;       // Implicit conversion: 65.0
double d = f;      // Implicit conversion: 65.0

// Narrowing (potentially unsafe, requires explicit cast in C++11)
double d = 3.14;
float f = d;       // Implicit (works, precision loss)
int i = f;         // Implicit (works, truncation: 3)
char c = i;        // Implicit (works, potential overflow)

// Better: explicit casts prevent mistakes
int i = static_cast<int>(3.14);  // Explicit
char c = static_cast<char>(200); // Clear intent, value might overflow
```

**C++ Cast Operators**:
```cpp
// 1. static_cast - Compile-time type conversions
double d = 3.14;
int i = static_cast<int>(d);  // 3

// 2. const_cast - Remove const (use sparingly)
const int x = 42;
int& ref = const_cast<int&>(x);  // Dangerous!
ref = 100;  // Undefined behavior

// 3. reinterpret_cast - Low-level reinterpretation (dangerous)
int i = 42;
int* ptr = &i;
long l = reinterpret_cast<long>(ptr);  // Converts address to long

// 4. dynamic_cast - Safe polymorphic cast (requires RTTI)
class Animal { virtual ~Animal() {} };
class Dog : public Animal {};

Animal* animal = new Dog();
Dog* dog = dynamic_cast<Dog*>(animal);
if (dog) {
    cout << "Successfully cast to Dog" << endl;
} else {
    cout << "Cast failed" << endl;
}

// 5. Old C-style casts (avoid in modern C++)
int i = (int)3.14;  // Does what the compiler thinks is best
```

