### References

**References Theory**:
- Reference is an alias to another variable
- Cannot be null, must be initialized
- Cannot be reassigned to refer to different variable
- Automatically dereferenced (no `*` needed)
- Compile-time abstraction (no runtime overhead)

```cpp
int x = 10;
int& ref = x;        // ref is a reference to x (alias)

// Using references
ref = 20;            // Modifies x
cout << x;           // 20

cout << ref;         // 20 (same as cout << x)
cout << &ref;        // Same address as &x (both refer to same variable)

// References must be initialized
int& bad_ref;        // ERROR - reference without initialization

// References cannot be rebound
int y = 30;
ref = y;             // Does NOT make ref refer to y
                     // Instead, assigns value of y to x (now x = 30)

int& ref2 = ref;     // ref2 is also a reference to x
```

**References vs Pointers Comparison**:
```cpp
int x = 10, y = 20;

// Pointer
int* ptr = &x;
ptr = &y;            // ptr now points to y (can rebind)
cout << *ptr;        // 20

// Reference
int& ref = x;
// ref = y;           // ERROR - cannot rebind reference
                      // Instead: ref = y assigns y's value to x
                      // Now x = 20, ref still refers to x

// Usage difference
cout << ptr;         // Prints address
cout << *ptr;        // Prints value (must dereference)

cout << ref;         // Prints value (no * needed)
cout << &ref;        // Prints address
```

**Comparison Table**:
| Feature | Pointer | Reference |
|---------|---------|-----------|
| Can be null | Yes | No |
| Can be reassigned | Yes | No |
| Dereferencing required | Yes (`*ptr`) | No (automatic) |
| Takes pointer space | Yes (8 bytes) | No (no storage) |
| Address-of operator | Yes (`&ptr`) | Yes (`&ref`, same as original) |
| Arithmetic | Yes (`ptr++`) | No |
| Can be const | Yes | Yes |
| Initialization | Optional | Required |

