## Templates

### Template Theory & Function Templates

**Theory**: Templates enable generic programming - write code once, works with any type.

**How Templates Work**:
```
Template Definition (source code)
    ↓
Template Instantiation (compiler generates code for each type)
    ↓
Specialized Functions/Classes (one for each type used)
    ↓
Object Code (compiled machine code)
```

**Key Points**:
- No runtime overhead (compile-time polymorphism)
- Larger executable (code for each type)
- Better optimization (compiler knows the type)
- Longer compilation (must instantiate all types)

```cpp
// Generic function template
template <typename T>
T add(T a, T b) {
    return a + b;  // T can be any type with + operator
}

// Usage
cout << add(5, 3);             // T = int, result = 8
cout << add(5.5, 3.2);         // T = double, result = 8.7
cout << add("Hello", "World"); // T = string, result = "HelloWorld"

// Compiler generates equivalent to:
int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }
string add(string a, string b) { return a + b; }
```

