## Advanced Features

### Move Semantics & Rvalue References (C++11)

**Theory**: Move semantics enable efficient transfer of resources instead of copying. Critical for performance.

```cpp
// Traditional copy (expensive)
string source = "Hello World";
string destination = source;  // Deep copy of entire string

// Move semantics (efficient)
string source = "Hello World";
string destination = move(source);  // Steals resources from source
// source is now empty, no deep copy

// Practical example
class Matrix {
private:
    vector<vector<double>> data;
public:
    // Copy constructor (expensive)
    Matrix(const Matrix& other) {
        data = other.data;  // Deep copy
    }
    
    // Move constructor (efficient)
    Matrix(Matrix&& other) noexcept {
        data = move(other.data);  // Steal data
        other.data.clear();       // Leave source empty
    }
    
    // Copy assignment
    Matrix& operator=(const Matrix& other) {
        if (this != &other) {
            data = other.data;  // Deep copy
        }
        return *this;
    }
    
    // Move assignment
    Matrix& operator=(Matrix&& other) noexcept {
        if (this != &other) {
            data = move(other.data);  // Steal data
            other.data.clear();
        }
        return *this;
    }
};

// Usage
Matrix createLargeMatrix() {
    Matrix m(1000, 1000);
    // ... fill with data
    return m;  // Move semantics prevents copy overhead
}

int main() {
    Matrix m = createLargeMatrix();  // No deep copy, uses move
}
```

**Lvalue vs Rvalue**:
```cpp
int x = 10;
int& lref = x;         // Lvalue reference: refers to named object

// int& rref = 42;      // ERROR: lvalue reference to rvalue

int&& rrref = 42;      // Rvalue reference: temporary value
int&& rrref2 = x + 1;  // Rvalue reference: expression result

// Perfect forwarding (C++11)
template<typename T>
void forward_func(T&& arg) {
    // Detects whether arg is lvalue or rvalue and forwards accordingly
    another_func(forward<T>(arg));
}
```

### Concepts & Type Traits (C++20)

**Concepts**: Enable constraints on template parameters.

```cpp
#include <concepts>

// Without concepts (hard to understand constraints)
template<typename T>
auto process(T val) {
    return val * 2;  // What types are valid here?
}

// With concepts (explicit constraints)
template<std::integral T>  // Only integer types
auto process(T val) {
    return val * 2;
}

// Custom concepts
template<typename T>
concept Addable = requires(T a, T b) {
    { a + b } -> std::convertible_to<T>;
};

template<Addable T>
T add(T a, T b) {
    return a + b;
}

// Usage
add(5, 3);           // OK: int is Addable
add(5.5, 3.3);       // OK: double is Addable
add("Hello", "World"); // ERROR: string doesn't satisfy Addable (+ returns different type)
```

### Coroutines (C++20)

**Theory**: Functions that can suspend and resume. Enable efficient async programming.

```cpp
#include <coroutine>
#include <iostream>

// Simple coroutine that yields values
class Generator {
public:
    struct promise_type {
        int current_value;
        
        Generator get_return_object() {
            return Generator(handle_type::from_promise(*this));
        }
        
        suspend_never initial_suspend() { return {}; }
        suspend_always final_suspend() noexcept { return {}; }
        
        suspend_always yield_value(int value) {
            current_value = value;
            return {};
        }
        
        void return_void() {}
        void unhandled_exception() {}
    };

    struct handle_type : public std::coroutine_handle<promise_type> {
        using std::coroutine_handle<promise_type>::coroutine_handle;
    };

    handle_type h;

    Generator(handle_type h) : h(h) {}

    ~Generator() {
        if (h) h.destroy();
    }

    bool next() {
        h.resume();
        return !h.done();
    }

    int value() const {
        return h.promise().current_value;
    }
};

// Coroutine function (automatic with co_yield)
Generator countUp() {
    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
        co_yield i;  // Suspend and return value
    }
}

int main() {
    auto gen = countUp();
    while (gen.next()) {
        std::cout << gen.value() << " ";  // 1 2 3 4 5
    }
}
```

### Modules (C++20)

**Theory**: Alternative to header/source file organization. Better encapsulation and faster compilation.

```cpp
// module.cpp (module implementation)
export module MyModule;

export namespace Math {
    int add(int a, int b);
    int multiply(int a, int b);
}

namespace Math {
    int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
    int multiply(int a, int b) { return a * b; }
}

// main.cpp (using the module)
import MyModule;
import <iostream>;

int main() {
    std::cout << Math::add(5, 3) << std::endl;         // 8
    std::cout << Math::multiply(5, 3) << std::endl;    // 15
}
```

### Attributes (C++11 onwards)

**Attributes**: Provide hints to compiler and tools.

```cpp
// [[noreturn]] - Function never returns
[[noreturn]] void error(const string& msg) {
    throw runtime_error(msg);
}

// [[nodiscard]] - Warn if return value ignored
[[nodiscard]] int calculate() {
    return 42;
}

int main() {
    calculate();  // WARNING: ignoring return value
}

// [[maybe_unused]] - Suppress unused variable warnings
