## Performance & Optimization

### Memory Optimization

**Stack vs Heap**:
```cpp
// Stack allocation (fast, automatic cleanup)
{
    int local = 42;              // ~nanoseconds
    double arr[1000];            // Allocated in nanoseconds
    vector<int> v = {1,2,3};    // Fixed capacity, fast
}  // All automatically freed

// Heap allocation (slower, manual cleanup)
{
    int* ptr = new int(42);      // ~microseconds
    double* arr = new double[1000]; // ~microseconds
    delete[] arr;                 // Must manually deallocate
    delete ptr;
}
```

**Optimization: Prefer Stack**:
```cpp
// Bad: Unnecessary heap allocation
class Widget {
    vector<int>* data;  // Indirection overhead
public:
    Widget() : data(new vector<int>) {}
    ~Widget() { delete data; }
};

// Good: Direct member
class Widget {
    vector<int> data;   // No indirection
public:
    Widget() {}         // RAII - automatic cleanup
};
```

### Cache Efficiency

**Example**: Data structure layout affects performance

```cpp
// Bad: Cache misses due to poor layout
struct BadLayout {
    char a;      // 1 byte (followed by 7 bytes padding)
    long long b; // 8 bytes
    char c;      // 1 byte (followed by 7 bytes padding)
    long long d; // 8 bytes
};  // Total: 32 bytes (with padding)

// Good: Better cache locality
struct GoodLayout {
    long long b; // 8 bytes
    long long d; // 8 bytes
    char a;      // 1 byte (followed by 7 bytes padding)
    char c;      // 1 byte (followed by 7 bytes padding)
};  // Total: 32 bytes, but better alignment

// Best: Packed structure
#pragma pack(1)
struct PackedLayout {
    char a;      // 1 byte
    char c;      // 1 byte
    long long b; // 8 bytes
    long long d; // 8 bytes
};  // Total: 18 bytes, but slower access
#pragma pack()
```

**Example: Cache-Friendly Iteration**:
```cpp
// Bad: Cache misses (column-major access)
vector<vector<int>> matrix(1000, vector<int>(1000));
for (int j = 0; j < 1000; j++) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
        sum += matrix[i][j];  // Jumps across memory (cache misses)
    }
}

// Good: Cache hits (row-major access)
for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 1000; j++) {
        sum += matrix[i][j];  // Sequential memory (cache hits)
    }
}  // 10-100x faster in practice
```

### String Optimization

```cpp
// Bad: Multiple allocations
string result = "";
for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
    result += to_string(i);  // Allocates new string each time
    result += ",";
}
// Result: 1,000,000+ allocations

// Good: Pre-allocate capacity
string result;
result.reserve(15000000);  // Allocate once
for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
    result += to_string(i);
    result += ",";
}
// Result: 1-3 allocations (amortized)

// Better: Use stringstream
stringstream ss;
for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
    ss << i << ",";
}
string result = ss.str();
// Result: Efficient buffering
```

### Algorithm Optimization

```cpp
// Big-O Complexity Comparison
vector<int> v = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6};

// O(n) - Linear search
auto it = find(v.begin(), v.end(), 5);  // Best: O(1), Avg: O(n), Worst: O(n)

// O(n log n) - Sorting
sort(v.begin(), v.end());  // All cases: O(n log n)

// O(n²) - Bubble sort (avoid)
for (int i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < v.size() - 1 - i; j++) {
        if (v[j] > v[j+1]) swap(v[j], v[j+1]);
    }
}

// Performance impact (n = 1,000,000):
// O(n) = 1 million operations = 1ms
// O(n log n) = 20 million operations = 20ms
// O(n²) = 1 trillion operations = 16 hours
```

